import{c,lesson}from'./extended-content.js';
import{PHI,GOLDEN_ANGLE,birthdayChance,collatz,harmonic,fibonacci,logistic,squareFourier,primeSpiral,montyOutcomes,complexProduct}from'./rare-math.js';
export const rareDiscoveries=[
lesson('birthday','The Shared-Day Comet','Birthday paradox','PROBABILITY','#f2be86','Only 23 people. Why is a match already likely?','Change the group size. Watch the chance of at least one shared birthday.',[c('n','People in the room',2,70,1,23)],'A match can occur between any pair, not just between you and somebody else. The number of possible pairs grows quickly. We assume independent, equally likely birthdays across 365 days.','P(match) = 1 − ∏(1 − k/365), k = 0…n−1','It is easier to count the complement: everybody has a different birthday. Person two avoids one date, person three avoids two, and so on. Multiply these conditional probabilities, then subtract from one. Real birthday distributions differ from this model.','In this model, the chance of a match among 23 people is…',['About 6%','Slightly more than 50%','Exactly 100%'],1,'The probability is about 50.7%. There are 253 distinct pairs in a group of 23.','combinations',v=>`${v.n} people form ${v.n*(v.n-1)/2} possible pairs. Probability of a shared birthday: ${(birthdayChance(v.n)*100).toFixed(2)}%.`),
lesson('monty','The Door That Lied','Monty Hall problem','PROBABILITY','#eebbd6','Why does switching doors win more often?','Choose an initial door. Inspect all three equally likely prize locations.',[c('pick','Your initial door',1,3,1,1)],'The host knows the prize location and always opens a different, losing door. Your original choice has a one-third chance. Switching wins whenever your original choice was wrong.','P(stay wins) = 1/3; P(switch wins) = 2/3','Enumerate the three equally likely prize locations. Staying succeeds in one case; switching succeeds in two. The host must always reveal a losing unchosen door and always offer the switch. A different host protocol can change the conclusion.','Under these host rules, should you switch?',['Yes: switching wins in 2 of 3 cases','No: staying wins in 2 of 3 cases','It is always a 50–50 choice'],0,'Switching inherits the probability that your original choice was wrong: two thirds.','probability',v=>{const cases=montyOutcomes(v.pick-1);return`Starting at door ${v.pick}: staying wins ${cases.filter(x=>x.stayWins).length}/3 cases; switching wins ${cases.filter(x=>x.switchWins).length}/3. The host is giving information, not removing it.`}),
lesson('collatz','The Unsolved Traveller','Collatz conjecture','NUMBER THEORY','#cbb0f4','Two simple rules. A question still without a general proof.','Choose a starting integer. Follow its sequence toward 1.',[c('seed','Starting integer',1,100,1,27)],'If the number is even, halve it. If it is odd, multiply by three and add one. Repeat. Every starting value offered here reaches 1, sometimes after an unexpectedly long trip.','Even: n/2. Odd: 3n + 1.','The statement that every positive integer eventually reaches 1 is the Collatz conjecture. A finite experiment cannot prove it for all integers. The app stops at 1, at a safety limit, or before unsafe integer arithmetic. The example from 27 rises far before descending.','Does testing every integer up to 100 prove the conjecture?',['Yes, because the rule is simple','No, it leaves infinitely many cases','Only if every sequence is short'],1,'Evidence for a finite set is not a proof for every positive integer.','logistic',v=>{const r=collatz(v.seed);return`Start ${v.seed}: ${r.steps} steps, peak ${r.peak}. ${r.reachedOne?'This example reaches 1.':'The safety limit was reached.'} This is evidence, not a universal proof.`}),
lesson('golden','The Seed of Order','Golden-angle patterns','GEOMETRY','#edcd83','How can one repeated turn create this pattern?','Change the angle between successive seeds. Compare 120°, 137.5°, and 180°.',[c('angle','Angle per seed',110,180,.1,137.5,'°'),c('n','Seeds',50,600,10,300)],'Each seed is placed farther from the center and turned by the same angle. Some angles produce obvious spokes. Near the golden angle, seeds avoid repeating a small set of directions.','θgolden = 360° / φ² ≈ 137.508°','The construction uses radius proportional to √n and angle nθ. Rational fractions of a turn repeat their directions; irrational fractions do not repeat exactly. This model resembles some plant arrangements, but it does not prove that every plant follows it.','At 180° per seed, how many directions repeat?',['Two','Three','Infinitely many distinct directions'],0,'Each turn alternates between two opposite directions.','fibonacci',v=>`${v.n} seeds, each turned ${v.angle.toFixed(1)}°. Golden angle: ${GOLDEN_ANGLE.toFixed(3)}°. This is a geometric model, not a universal biological rule.`),
lesson('euler','The Unchanging Count','Euler’s polyhedron formula','TOPOLOGY','#9ddbd5','Why does this count stay at two?','Change the number of sides of a prism. Count its vertices, edges, and faces.',[c('n','Sides of the prism base',3,10,1,5)],'The shape changes, but vertices minus edges plus faces stays at two for these convex polyhedra. A triangle-based prism and a ten-sided prism obey the same count.','V − E + F = 2','For an n-sided prism, V=2n, E=3n, and F=n+2, giving 2n−3n+n+2=2. More generally, convex polyhedra have this Euler characteristic. Surfaces with holes require different values; this prism calculation is not a proof for every surface.','A cube has 8 vertices and 12 edges. How many faces?', ['4','6','8'],1,'8−12+F=2 gives F=6.','pythagoras',v=>`Vertices ${2*v.n}; edges ${3*v.n}; faces ${v.n+2}. ${2*v.n} − ${3*v.n} + ${v.n+2} = 2.`),
lesson('harmonic','The Slow Infinity','Harmonic series','CALCULUS','#eca89b','The pieces approach zero. Why does their sum keep growing?','Increase the number of terms. Compare the partial sum with a geometric series.',[c('n','Terms included',1,1000,1,32)],'The terms 1, 1/2, 1/3, … get smaller, yet their sum has no finite upper bound. Terms approaching zero are necessary for convergence, but not sufficient.','Hₙ = 1 + 1/2 + … + 1/n','Group terms in blocks: (1/3+1/4) is at least 1/2; (1/5+…+1/8) is at least 1/2; every doubled block contributes at least 1/2. Infinitely many such blocks force divergence. The finite chart only illustrates this argument.','If the terms approach zero, must a series converge?',['Yes','No','Only when the terms are positive'],1,'The harmonic series is a counterexample: its terms approach zero while the partial sums grow without bound.','limits',v=>`${v.n} terms total ${harmonic(v.n).toFixed(4)}. Growth is slow, but no finite ceiling exists. Small contributions can still add up forever.`),
lesson('primes','The Hidden Diagonals','Ulam prime spiral','NUMBER THEORY','#b0d797','Do primes draw patterns?','Increase the number of integers in the square spiral. Only primes glow.',[c('n','Integers in the spiral',49,961,1,441)],'Write positive integers in an outward square spiral and highlight the primes. Diagonal streaks become visible. A compelling visual pattern is a starting point for questions, not a proof that a whole line is prime.','Prime: an integer > 1 with exactly two positive divisors','This display checks primality with a sieve and places every integer in order. Certain diagonals follow quadratic expressions. Some produce many primes over a range, but a visible streak does not establish that the expression always gives primes.','Does a glowing diagonal prove every later point on it is prime?',['Yes','No','Only if the diagonal is long'],1,'Finite visual evidence cannot establish an infinite claim. Each candidate still needs justification.','modular',v=>`${primeSpiral(v.n).filter(p=>p.prime).length} primes among the first ${v.n} integers. One is not prime.`),
lesson('pigeonhole','The Overbooked Asteroid','Pigeonhole principle','DISCRETE MATH','#ddb7ef','When is sharing unavoidable?','Add visitors and rooms. The arrangement spreads visitors as evenly as possible.',[c('rooms','Rooms',2,8,1,4),c('visitors','Visitors',1,25,1,9)],'If more visitors than rooms each need a room, at least one room must contain two or more visitors. More generally, one room holds at least the ceiling of visitors divided by rooms.','At least one room has ≥ ⌈N / k⌉ visitors','If every room held fewer than ⌈N/k⌉ visitors, the total capacity would be less than N. That contradiction proves the bound. The balanced picture demonstrates the smallest achievable maximum occupancy.','With 9 visitors and 4 rooms, one room must contain at least…',['2','3','4'],1,'If every room held at most two, only eight visitors would fit.','combinations',v=>`${v.visitors} visitors in ${v.rooms} rooms force a room with at least ${Math.ceil(v.visitors/v.rooms)}. No reservation system can negotiate with that.`),
lesson('fibonacci','The Ratio Beacon','Fibonacci ratios','NUMBER THEORY','#edce86','How does an irrational ratio emerge from whole numbers?','Increase the sequence index. Watch the ratio of consecutive Fibonacci numbers.',[c('n','Sequence index n',2,20,1,8)],'Each Fibonacci number is the sum of the previous two. Ratios of neighboring positive terms oscillate around a value they approach: the golden ratio.','Fₙ₊₁/Fₙ → φ = (1 + √5)/2','A positive limiting ratio L must satisfy L=1+1/L, whose positive solution is φ. This equation identifies a candidate; convergence can be established using the explicit Fibonacci formula and the decay of its second exponential term.','Which equation is satisfied by the golden ratio?',['φ² = φ + 1','φ² = 2φ','φ = 3/2'],0,'The positive root of x²−x−1=0 is (1+√5)/2.','golden',v=>{const f=fibonacci(v.n+1);return`${f[v.n+1]} / ${f[v.n]} = ${(f[v.n+1]/f[v.n]).toFixed(6)}. Golden ratio ${PHI.toFixed(6)}.`}),
lesson('logistic','The Butterfly Frontier','Deterministic chaos','DYNAMICAL SYSTEMS','#dca8d9','Can almost identical starts lead to different futures?','Compare two starting values separated by 0.0001. Change the growth parameter.',[c('r','Growth parameter r',2.5,4,.01,3.9),c('x','Initial value',.1,.8,.01,.4)],'A deterministic rule can amplify tiny differences in initial conditions. Some parameter values settle; others produce complicated behavior. The system is deterministic even when long-term prediction is difficult.','xₙ₊₁ = r xₙ (1 − xₙ)','The logistic map keeps values in [0,1] for the displayed range. Its slope r(1−2x) can magnify small differences locally. Repeated stretching and folding creates sensitivity in chaotic regimes. Not every r in this range is chaotic, and the finite plot does not prove chaos.','Are the two sequences using random steps?',['Yes','No: both follow the same fixed rule','Only after they diverge'],1,'Both use the same deterministic formula. Their initial values differ slightly.','collatz',v=>{const a=logistic(v.r,v.x,60),b=logistic(v.r,v.x+.0001,60);return`After 60 steps, the difference is ${Math.abs(a[60]-b[60]).toFixed(6)}. Try r=2.8, then r=3.9. Different parameters can have very different behavior.`}),
lesson('complex','The Imaginary Compass','Complex multiplication','COMPLEX NUMBERS','#9cd2ee','What does multiplying by i actually do?','Change the multiplier’s angle and size. Watch one vector rotate and stretch.',[c('angle','Multiplier angle',0,360,1,90,'°'),c('scale','Multiplier magnitude',.5,2,.1,1)],'A complex number can represent a point or vector in a plane. Multiplication adds angles and multiplies magnitudes. Multiplying by i rotates a vector by 90° counterclockwise.','reⁱθ · seⁱφ = rs eⁱ⁽θ⁺φ⁾','Expand (a+bi)(c+di) using i²=−1 to obtain (ac−bd)+(ad+bc)i. This is the coordinate rule for rotation and scaling. The polar form describes the same operation through lengths and angles.','Multiplying by i twice is equivalent to…',['No change','A 180° rotation, multiplying by −1','Doubling the length'],1,'i²=−1, so two quarter-turns produce a half-turn without changing length.','unitcircle',v=>{const p=complexProduct(2,1,v.scale,v.angle);return`2+i becomes ${p[0].toFixed(2)} ${p[1]<0?'−':'+'} ${Math.abs(p[1]).toFixed(2)}i. Length scales by ${v.scale.toFixed(1)}; direction turns ${v.angle}°.`}),
lesson('fourier','The Shape of Sound','Fourier synthesis','HARMONIC ANALYSIS','#92e1d4','Can smooth waves build a sharp-edged shape?','Add odd harmonics. Watch the sum approach a square wave.',[c('terms','Odd harmonics',1,15,1,5)],'Combine sine waves with frequencies 1, 3, 5, … and progressively smaller amplitudes. Their sum builds a shape very different from any one wave. More terms sharpen the transitions.','(4/π) Σ sin((2k+1)x)/(2k+1)','This Fourier series converges to the square wave away from its jumps and to the midpoint at each jump. Near a jump, a persistent overshoot remains as terms increase: the Gibbs phenomenon. A finite sum is always smooth, never a perfect discontinuity.','Do finitely many sine waves produce an exact discontinuous square wave?',['Yes','No: a finite sum remains continuous','Only with an even number of terms'],1,'Every sine wave is continuous, and a finite sum of continuous functions remains continuous.','waves',v=>`${v.terms} odd harmonics, up to frequency ${2*v.terms-1}. The sharp transitions improve, but the overshoot near jumps does not simply vanish.`)
];
const sourceById={birthday:'BirthdayProblem',monty:'MontyHallProblem',collatz:'CollatzConjecture',golden:'GoldenAngle',euler:'EulerCharacteristic',harmonic:'HarmonicSeries',primes:'PrimeSpiral',fibonacci:'FibonacciNumber',logistic:'LogisticMap',complex:'EulerFormula'};
for(const d of rareDiscoveries){d.rare=['birthday','monty','collatz','golden','euler','harmonic','primes','pigeonhole'].includes(d.id);if(sourceById[d.id])d.source={title:'Wolfram MathWorld',url:`https://mathworld.wolfram.com/${sourceById[d.id]}.html`}}
